<<<
PERIOD IN ABBREVIATIONS. The practice of ending every abbreviation with a period (Wm. for William as well as Gen. for General, viz. for videlicet as well as sc. for scilicet, qr. for quarter as well as lb. for libra) is ill advised.
===
**PERIOD IN ABBREVIATIONS.** The practice of ending every abbreviation with a period (*Wm.* for William as well as *Gen.* for General, ^^VIZ^^. for videlicet as well as *sc.* for scilicet, *qr.* for quarter as well as *lb.* for libra) is ill advised.
>>>

<<<
writing wt (not wt.) for weight, Bp (not Bp.) for bishop, Mr (not Mr.) for Mister, Bart (not Bart.) for baronet, bot. for botany but bot for bought, Capt. for captain but Cpl for corporal, doz. for dozen but cwt for hundredweight,
===
writing *wt* (not *wt.*) for weight, *Bp* (not *Bp.*) for bishop, *Mr* (not *Mr.*) for Mister, *Bart* (not *Bart.*) for baronet, *bot.* for botany but *bot* for bought, *Capt.* for captain but *Cpl* for corporal, *doz.* for dozen but *cwt* for hundredweight,
>>>

<<<
Feb. for February but fep for foolscap, Frl. for Fraulein but Mlle for Mademoiselle, in. for inches but ft for feet, Geo. for George but Thos for Thomas, Lat. for Latin but Gk for Greek, h.w., but ht wt, for hit wicket.
===
*Feb.* for February but *fcp* for foolscap, *Frl.* for Fräulein but *Mlle* for Mademoiselle, *in.* for inches but *ft* for feet, *Geo.* for George but *Thos* for Thomas, *Lat.* for Latin but *Gk* for Greek, *h.w.*, but *ht wt*, for hit wicket.
>>>

<<<
periodic(al). The -ic form is not used of publications (periodical literature, periodicals); the -ical form is not used of literary composition (Johnson’s periodic style); otherwise the two words do not differ in meaning, but the longer tends to oust the shorter.
===
**periodic(al).** The *-ic* form is not used of publications (*periodical literature, periodicals*); the *-ical* form is not used of literary composition (*Johnson's periodic style*); otherwise the two words do not differ in meaning, but the longer tends to oust the shorter.
>>>

